WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.292
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.592 --> 00:00:09.060
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:16.029
Unit sixteen: Comparative History. Today you will identify patterns between countries with 'across' and 'throughout'.

4
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:19.005
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:23.107
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.323
Comparative history.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:31.323
Comparative history means the study of similarities and differences between societies' pasts.

8
00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:35.530
Comparative history examines several revolutions together.

9
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:39.466
Your turn. Say: Comparative history.

10
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.812
Comparative history.

11
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.795
Cross-national.

12
00:00:46.783 --> 00:00:49.897
Cross-national means involving several countries.

13
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.412
The cross-national study included ten states.

14
00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:57.238
Your turn. Say: Cross-national.

15
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.426
Cross-national.

16
00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:02.964
Pattern.

17
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:07.006
Pattern means a regular and repeated way something happens.

18
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:10.522
A pattern of land reform followed independence.

19
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:13.595
Your turn. Say: Pattern.

20
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:17.260
Pattern.

21
00:01:18.017 --> 00:01:19.145
Divergence.

22
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.939
Divergence means a process of becoming different.

23
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.964
The two economies show striking divergence after 1960.

24
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.017
Your turn. Say: Divergence.

25
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:34.763
Divergence.

26
00:01:35.517 --> 00:01:36.785
Convergence.

27
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:40.654
Convergence means a process of becoming similar.

28
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.679
Convergence occurred as trade increased.

29
00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:46.994
Your turn. Say: Convergence.

30
00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:50.997
Convergence.

31
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:52.693
Case study.

32
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:57.099
Case study means a detailed examination of one example.

33
00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.869
Cambodia serves as a case study.

34
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:03.147
Your turn. Say: Case study.

35
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.048
Case study.

36
00:02:07.783 --> 00:02:09.063
Generalization.

37
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.861
Generalization means a broad statement based on several examples.

38
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.332
Avoid generalizations based on one country.

39
00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:20.465
Your turn. Say: Generalization.

40
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:24.545
Generalization.

41
00:02:25.283 --> 00:02:26.273
Context.

42
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:30.920
Context means the specific circumstances of a place and time.

43
00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:34.282
Every case must be understood in context.

44
00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:37.559
Your turn. Say: Context.

45
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.587
Context.

46
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.865
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:51.726
Visal, you compared independence movements in Southeast Asia. What did you find?

48
00:02:52.817 --> 00:03:01.831
Patterns emerge across many different national histories. Throughout the region, nationalist leaders were often educated in colonial schools.

49
00:03:02.917 --> 00:03:04.254
And the differences?

50
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:15.584
There was strong divergence in the methods. Cambodia achieved independence largely through negotiation, while Vietnam and Indonesia fought long wars.

51
00:03:16.683 --> 00:03:18.486
Why does comparison matter?

52
00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:28.180
It tests generalizations. Across all these cases, context explains why similar causes produced different outcomes.

53
00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:30.741
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:35.197
Grammar focus: Across and throughout prepositions.

55
00:03:35.497 --> 00:03:36.742
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.552
Patterns emerge across many different national histories.

57
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.365
Nationalist leaders were educated in colonial schools throughout the region.

58
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.352
Similar reforms spread throughout the twentieth century.

59
00:03:50.300 --> 00:04:01.915
'Across' suggests comparison between separate units (across countries, across cultures). 'Throughout' means in every part of an area or for the whole of a period (throughout the region, throughout the 1950s).

60
00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:07.543
Be careful! Don't say: Patterns emerge across of many histories.

61
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.881
Say: Patterns emerge across many histories.

62
00:04:13.181 --> 00:04:14.577
'Across' needs no 'of'.

63
00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.798
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.274
Why Compare Histories?.

65
00:04:20.574 --> 00:04:26.858
Comparative history places two or more societies side by side to understand both their similarities and their differences.

66
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:35.207
Across Southeast Asia, for example, colonial rule produced comparable economic structures based on exporting raw materials.

67
00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.462
Throughout the region, however, independence was achieved in very different ways and at different speeds.

68
00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:48.820
Comparison allows historians to test generalizations that might seem convincing when only one case is examined.

69
00:04:49.833 --> 00:04:55.560
At the same time, careful attention to context prevents comparison from becoming oversimplification.

70
00:04:56.667 --> 00:05:01.040
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.883 --> 00:05:04.800
Rice farming is practised blank the whole region.

72
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.742
A, throughout. B, across of. C, along.

73
00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.183
The answer is A: throughout.

74
00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.406
The study compared attitudes blank ten countries.

75
00:05:18.706 --> 00:05:22.394
A, across. B, throughout of. C, over of.

76
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:27.794
The answer is A: across.

77
00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.228
A process of becoming different is blank.

78
00:05:31.528 --> 00:05:35.878
A, convergence. B, divergence. C, pattern.

79
00:05:39.433 --> 00:05:42.060
The answer is B: divergence.

80
00:05:43.217 --> 00:05:46.493
A detailed examination of one example is a blank.

81
00:05:46.793 --> 00:05:51.004
A, case study. B, generalization. C, context.

82
00:05:54.567 --> 00:05:57.147
The answer is A: case study.

83
00:05:58.283 --> 00:06:01.281
According to the reading, comparison helps historians to…

84
00:06:01.581 --> 00:06:07.127
A, avoid context. B, test generalizations. C, study only one case.

85
00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:13.967
The answer is B: test generalizations.

86
00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:18.214
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:22.663
What pattern have you noticed across the histories of different countries?

88
00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:40.950
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:41.250 --> 00:07:15.441
One pattern across many countries' histories is that periods of rapid economic change are often followed by political unrest. Throughout the twentieth century, for instance, industrialization and urban growth created new social groups that demanded political rights. In South Korea and Taiwan, economic development was eventually followed by democratic movements. However, some countries remained authoritarian despite growth, so context always matters.

90
00:07:15.741 --> 00:07:17.369
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:18.283 --> 00:07:22.863
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and across and throughout prepositions.

92
00:07:23.163 --> 00:07:28.416
Today's value is global perspective. Local stories can carry universal truths.

93
00:07:28.716 --> 00:07:31.496
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:31.796 --> 00:07:35.284
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

